              "content": "Fig. 4.4: A geometrical model representing the identity $(a+b+c)^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+2ab+2bc+2ca$"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_chart_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.388,
            0.399,
            0.411
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.425,
            0.854,
            0.464
          ],
          "content": "Label the squares and rectangles in Fig. 4.4 so that it represents the identity $(a+b+c)^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+2ab+2bc+2ca.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.478,
            0.884,
            0.518
          ],
          "content": "**Example 9:** Let us use this identity to find the square of a number, say 119:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.522,
            0.73,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}119^{2} &= (100 + 10 + 9)^{2} \\\\&= 100^{2} + 10^{2} + 9^{2} + 2(100)(10) + 2(100)(9) + 2(10)(9) \\\\&= 10000 + 100 + 81 + 2000 + 1800 + 180 = 14161.\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.597,
            0.884,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": "So far we have verified the following three identities and used them for performing calculations and manipulating algebraic expressions:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.64,
            0.403,
            0.66
          ],
          "content": "1. $(a+b)^{2}=a^{2}+2ab+b^{2}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.664,
            0.401,
            0.683
          ],
          "content": "2. $(a - b)^{2} = a^{2} - 2ab + b^{2}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.687,
            0.603,
            0.708
          ],
          "content": "3. $(a+b+c)^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+2ab+2bc+2ca$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.375,
            0.732,
            0.622,
            0.761
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Exercise Set 4.3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.78,
            0.885,
            0.901
          ],
          "content": "1. Find the following squares using one of the above identities. Determine which of these identities will make these calculations easier. (i) $1 1 7 ^ { 2 }$ (ii) $7 8 ^ { 2 }$ (iii) $1 9 8 ^ { 2 }$ (iv) $2 1 4 ^ { 2 }$ (v) $1 1 0 4 ^ { 2 }$ (vi) $1 1 2 0 ^ { 2 }$"
        }
      ]
    }
